= To Be a #emph[Poseq]\ Hakham Ben Ṣion Meʾir Ḥai ʿUzziel #firstFootnote[\[Passages in square brackets in the text and footnotes are additions by the translator.\]] \
<to-be-a-poseq-hakham-ben-ṣion-meʾir-ḥai-ʿuzziel>
 
#v(1em)
#align(right)[#emph[From the Introduction to Mishpaṭe ʿUzziel,\ Volume 1, Tel Aviv 1935, pp. viii-xi.\ Translation: Simon Montagu.]]
#v(1em)

As I come to publish this little book, I know that I will be asked whom
the work is expected to benefit. The learned Rabbis and Torah scholars
of our time have before them hundreds and thousands of works of responsa
literature by the rabbis of every previous generation, and they will
rightly say “Are you bringing straw to ʿAfarayim?”#footnote[#emph[Menaḥot] 85a \[equivalent to the English expression “bringing coals to
Newcastle”\].] Or if the general public is the target audience, “anyone
inquiring for God’s word will go to the Tent of Meeting”#footnote[\[Based
on Exodus 33:7.\]] – to the Bet Midrash of contemporary rabbis who will
give them a practical halachic ruling on what is pure or impure,
forbidden or permitted, according to the breadth of their understanding
and their comprehensive and in-depth knowledge.

On the other hand, I realise that many will criticise what I have
written from different perspectives. Some will say “What have the rabbis
ever done for us? They have never ruled that the raven is permitted or
that the dove is forbidden.”#footnote[#emph[Sanhedrin] 99b.] This
is what lovers of change for its own sake said in earlier generations,
and the advocates of reforms in our own time have exactly the same
reaction to all compositions that say nothing new but only draw water
from the original and eternal spring.

Others will criticise me from the opposite perspective: “You were
lenient where you should have been strict.”; “You were strict where you
should have been lenient.”; “You were long-winded where you should have
been brief.”; “You were concise where you should have gone into detail.”

To all these questions I make the same brief and clear response: “I gain
understanding from the elders, since I have observed Your
commandments.”#footnote[\[Psalm 119:100.\]] This is what the early sages
and elders taught us, when they expounded on the verse “\[Let my
teaching fall like rain and my words descend like dew,\] like showers on
the grass”:#footnote[\[Deuteronomy 32:2.\]] “Just as showers fall on the
grass and run over them so that they do not rot away, so should you run
over the words of the Torah so that you do not forget them. As R.
Yaʿacob bar Ḥanina said to Ribbi \[Yehuda Ha-Nasi\], ‘Let us run over
the halachot and prevent them from rusting.’ ”#footnote[Sifre Haʾazinu
2.] This methodology has passed down from one generation to the next,
and it is what has brought into being the wealth of halachic writings
from every age and from every period, which is not equalled in the legal
literature of any other nation on earth.

A Talmudic judge or #emph[poseq] \[halachic decisor\] can not, and may
not, say to himself or to his questioners in response to any question he
is asked, “I will take down a book and consult it, and rule according to
everything I find already written there.” This is not the way of our
teachers: they are required to dig into the source of the halacha, to
investigate it thoroughly, refine it, sift it, and clarify it according
to comprehensive knowledge, accurate comparisons and correct reasoning,
and to reach a true conclusion which is faithful to the halacha. Any one
who decides halacha from what he has studied without conducting an
independent investigation to understand the halacha in depth is called
by our sages a “destroyer of the world”#footnote[#emph[Soṭa]
22a.];. As the ROSH#footnote[\[Rabbenu Asher ben Yeḥiʾel, 1250 or
1259–1327, Franco-Germany and Spain.\]] wrote, “When two great
authorities give opposing halachic rulings, the judge should not say, ‘I
will rule according to the one I prefer’. To do so is committing
perjury, and he is only entitled to do so if he is a great sage who has
studied and thought the issues through and is capable of deciding in
favour of one opinion with clear proofs. In that case, even if another
sage has ruled differently, the sage can adduce arguments against his
position and differ from him … all the more so if he has the support of
another side in the dispute. If he does not have this level of
competence, he is not entitled to make a financial ruling in a disputed
case.”#footnote[ROSH #emph[Sanhedrin] chapter 4 section 6, see also
#emph[SA Ḥoshen Mishpaṭ] 25:2 in the ReMA.]

In any case, a #emph[poseq] is required to know and investigate every
detail of the halacha from its sources and reasons, to make comparisons,
draw conclusions. and infer one thing from another, so that the issues
are clear to him. After all this, if he succeeds in coming to a
conclusive ruling based on clear knowledge and awareness, he can be
satisfied with a job well done.

This process of “halachic give and take,” as it is called, is like the
dew from heaven that descends on blades of grass and runs over them,
preventing them from rotting or becoming like dried up hay without
fresh, vital, and fruitful moisture. This is our obligation: to run over
the halachot, teach them orally and in writing, every one according to
his own style, his own understanding, his own reasoning, and his own
conclusions, and thus to prevent them from rusting and keep them
constantly fresh.

Moreover, the conditions of life, changes in values, and technical and
scientific discoveries create in every generation new questions and
problems that call out for solutions. We cannot ignore these questions
and say “Anything new is forbidden by the Torah,” meaning that anything
not explicitly mentioned by our predecessors counts as forbidden; still
less can we rule leniently according to our own opinion, and neither can
we leave the questions vague and undetermined and allow everyone to act
as they please. We have the obligation to go into the halacha in detail
and infer the implicit from the explicit, as our Sages said: “When
someone is brought to judgement he is asked, ‘Were you trustworthy in
business? … Did you engage in erudite discussions? Did you infer one
thing from another?’ ”#footnote[#emph[Shabbat] 31a.] They also
said things like “This Mishna was taught in the days of Nehemiah the son
of Hacaliah”,#footnote[#emph[Shabbat] 123b.] or “This Mishna was
taught in the days of Ribbi \[Yehuda Ha-Nasi\].”#footnote[#emph[Baba Qama] 94b, and Tosafot s.v. בימי. \[In both cases the Gemara’s
point is that these teachings only applied in the generation that they
were stated, and were not intended to be established halacha.\]] In this
context too, I say “I gain understanding from the elders, since I have
observed Your commandments,” i.e. when I learn from the Torah of the
elders and follow in their footsteps, I know that I have observed Your
commandments.

This awareness is what has compelled me to investigate most of the
questions in this little book and to search for solutions in the
teachings of my predecessors. In cases where the outstanding figures of
our generations and those that came before us had also discussed these
questions, I felt a personal obligation to make my own response
according to my limited understanding.

I have not made any innovations, and indeed we are not entitled to make
innovations, since even prophets through whom God’s spirit speaks and
who have His word on their lips are not entitled to make any
innovations, as our Sages wrote: “ ‘These are the commandments that He
commanded Moses’#footnote[Leviticus 27:34.] – this teaches us that no
prophet is entitled to make any innovations from that point
onwards.”#footnote[#emph[Shabbat] 104a.] Nor am I establishing
halacha for future generations, since studying texts and determining the
halacha that they lay down depends not on wisdom but on Divine
assistance.#footnote[#emph[Megilla] 6b.] Our sages also
interpreted the expression “God is with him”#footnote[\[I Samuel 16:18,
referring to David.\]] as meaning “halacha follows his opinion in every
case”.#footnote[#emph[Sanhedrin] 93a.] In all my responsa my
intention has not been to be lenient or stringent on the basis of my own
opinion or my own determination: my purpose has been to seek for and
discover the truth. As far as my understanding reaches I have followed
the light cast by our earlier teachers, whose waters we drink and whose
light illuminates us. I have lit up my eyes with this sacred light,
whose source is in the hidden and secret light \[of the first day of
Creation\]. I have approached each question in all its details with
reverence for holiness and respect for truth, with a profound prayer on
my lips taken from the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory: “May it be
Your will that what I say will be accepted.”#footnote[#emph[Beṣa]
38a.]

In all my responsa I have avoided as far as possible erudite
discussion, raising and answering hair-splitting problems that do
not affect the bottom line of the halacha. Although we are familiar with
the rabbinic saying that “God rejoices in erudite discussions of Torah,”
I prefer to rely on the Sages’ statement#footnote[\[Quoted above,
footnote 10.\]] which juxtaposes praise of erudite discussion and of
drawing logical inferences, and to conclude from it that erudite
discussion is valuable and desirable – if it is used for the purpose of
drawing inferences to support the correct halachic conclusion.

This has always been my methodology in my writings: to investigate,
examine and explain the words of our predecessors in order to reach a
halachic conclusion.

I have endeavoured to write clearly, relying on the reader to check my
sources and determine the truth and correctness of my interpretation.

Furthermore, I have divided every question into sections and subsections
and investigated each subsection individually so that every reader of
this book who is not satisfied with the conclusions of a responsum as a
whole, will be able to check the details of each subsection and perhaps
gain benefit from this study.

Here I feel an obligation to make a general observation: the responsa of
mine in this little book are only a suggestion of investigation into the
halacha, not practical halachic rulings, neither lasting nor immediate.
The Torah does not permit anyone to make definitive determinations of
halachic questions, except for the Supreme Bet Din, or an assembly of
the great rabbis of each generation who gather to determine halacha,
debate the questions and follow the decision of the majority by mutual
consent. Only such a Bet Din has the authority to make halachic rulings
that will obligate an entire generation.#footnote[See RaMBaM
#emph[Mishne Tora] #emph[Hilchot Mamrim] 1:5 and 5:1, and the SHaCH’s
essay on halachic procedural rules \[הנהגות הוראות אסור והיתר\] in his
notes on #emph[Shulḥan ʿArukh Yore Deʿa] 242.]

In particular I would like to comment here on what I wrote in my
responsum \(#emph[Oraḥ Ḥayyim] 9) on the question of riding in steam- or
electric-powered carriages on Shabbat. I wish to clarify that that
responsum was written to a questioner from outside Israel, where it is
not the case that public transport is operated for Jews. However, in the
land of Israel our community is growing and spreading throughout the
country, and all methods of transport used by non-Jews and operated by
non-Jews only exist in order to serve the Jewish community, who are the
principal customers of commercial transport throughout the country. Here
there is no way to permit Jews to travel by train or bus, even if the
drivers are not Jewish and even if most, or even all, of the passengers
are not Jewish, since the main reason that all these forms of transport
exist is for the benefit of the Jews.

Before I conclude, I also feel an obligation to mention at the beginning
of my book the pleasant and beloved memory of my friend and colleague
the distinguished and saintly rabbi Shelomo Hacohen Aharonson of blessed
memory, the chief rabbi of Tel Aviv and environs, who passed away on 21
Adar Sheni 5795 before he fulfilled the wish that he expressed to me
several times, of writing an introduction to this book. May his memory
be a blessing and may his soul attain eternal life.

After this brief introduction, I present my little book for the perusal
of my masters and teachers, the distinguished rabbis and their students,
and to the general public. I give thanks and blessings to those who
praise my work, and I pray to God, my strength and fortress, saying “May
I dwell in your tents for ever, Master of the Universe! May it be your
will that people quote my teachings in this world.”#footnote[#emph[Yebamot] 97a.] If I merit this, it will be the greatest reward I can ask
for.

Finally, I express my thanks and deep appreciation to the community of
Jaffa and Tel Aviv and all the adjacent communities for their great
generosity in appointing me to the position that I hold in peace and
tranquility. May God be with you, honoured and illustrious communities,
and may He grant you success in all your endeavours to spread our
settlement into every corner of the land, to raise the reputation of the
Torah of Israel and the Nation of Israel in the land of their ancestral
heritage, and may we all behold the redemption of the Jewish people from
the lands of its dispersion and its permanent establishment in the land
of its ancestral heritage, and the fulfilment of God’s purpose as spoken
by his prophet: “Many peoples will come and say, ‘Come, let us go up to
the mountain of the #smallcaps[Lord];, to the temple of the God of
Jacob. He will teach us his ways, so that we may walk in his paths.’ The
law will go out from Zion, the word of the #smallcaps[Lord] from
Jerusalem.”#footnote[Isaiah 2:3; Micah 4:2.]

